# A value that comes from a header and is written nowhere in this file
# (design.md §4.19, panel 038).
#
# Every number printed below is `math.h`'s or `stdio.h`'s. Nothing here can be
# copied wrong, because nothing here is copied: the emitter reads the name where
# the preprocessor can see it, and clang checks its type against the real header
# with the same `_Static_assert` an `extern function` gets.
#
# Two headers on purpose. The `#include` walk used to ask `FnKind` whether a
# declaration came from a header — and an `extern constant` lowers to
# `FnKind::Constant`, not `FnKind::Extern`, so a group holding *only* constants
# would have had its `#include` silently dropped. `stdio.h` here holds no
# function, which is the shape that catches it.
#
# It is also the case that makes the double-emit determinism test non-vacuous for
# this form: that test walks `tests/golden/emit` and `tests/golden/run` only
# (`golden.rs`), so before this file the accessor and its two assertions were
# emitted by nothing the test could see (panel 038, compiler-engineer's condition).

extern "math.h"
    constant M_PI: f64
    function sqrt(x: f64) -> f64

extern "stdio.h"
    constant SEEK_SET: i64
    constant SEEK_END: i64

function main()
    # `3.14159…` is in `math.h`, and the program says only its name.
    print(M_PI)
    print(sqrt(M_PI * M_PI))
    # Two `i64` macros, and the arithmetic is the language's own.
    print(SEEK_SET)
    print(SEEK_END - SEEK_SET)
